rebuild britain after coronavirus but  with dramatic changes that's the call  from 300 leading business charity and  religious leaders they want an overhaul  of the economy an end to inequality and  a 30 billion pound focus on  environmental policies like retrofitting  homes across the country to make them  energy-efficient something architect  Robert Pruitt spent thousands of his own  pounds doing almost a quarter of our co2  emissions result from just us being in  homes deep retrofit to hundreds of  thousands millions of homes would start  to reduce that dramatically down to only  a few percent of our co2 emissions the  call comes as a survey found that only  6% of people want things to return to  the way they were before coronavirus  with no changes 81% want government  created jobs for those who lost theirs  during the crisis and more than half say  tackling climate change now needs to be  a priority what's really interesting is  that this isn't about left or right and  actually organizations from across the  political spectrum with diverse views  have all come together and they are  saying we must build back better there  is a new common sense emerging there is  a new consensus emerging and actually  the public are with us 60% of the public  want to see changes in how they economy  is run there is significant support for  a so-called green recovery the chairs of  to House of Commons committees have  written to the Chancellor warning time  is running out to avert an even greater  future global crisis caused by climate  change other policies being discussed by  the 300 include a national care service  and minimum income protection the prime  minister has promised to spend big to  help Britain recover but he'll need a  hundred billion and a radical change of  policy to achieve these demands Sam  Holder Sky News
 

